  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that a linear congruence $ax≡b\\,(\\text{mod}\\,n)$ has a unique solution modulo $n$ if and only if $\\text{gcd}(a,n)=1.$ \n\nIn the given congruence, we have $a=17$ and $n=50$, where\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(a,n)=\\text{gcd}(17,50)=1.\n$$\n\nSo, our congruence does indeed have a unique solution.\n\nTo find the solution, we need to multiply both sides of the equation by the multiplicative inverse of $17\\,(\\text{mod}\\,50).$ To find the inverse of $17\\,(\\text{mod}\\,50)$, use the extended Euclidean algorithm.\n\nFirst, we apply the forward reduction:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}50 & = & 17\\cdot 2 & + & 16 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 17 & = & 16\\cdot 1 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving for the rightmost terms in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}16 & =50-17\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =17-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =17-16 \\\\  & =17-(50-17\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =17\\cdot 3-50\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can write this result in modulo $50,$ as follows:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17\\cdot 3-50 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,50) \\\\ 17\\cdot 3 & ≡1 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,50)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $17\\cdot 3≡1\\,(\\text{mod}\\,50),$ which means that $3$ is the multiplicative inverse of $17$ (modulo $50$).\n\nNow, multiplying both sides of our original congruence by $3,$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}17x & ≡9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,50) \\\\ 3\\cdot 17x & ≡3\\cdot 9 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,50) \\\\ (3\\cdot 17)x & ≡27 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,50) \\\\ 1\\cdot x & ≡27 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,50) \\\\ x & ≡27 &  & (\\text{mod}\\,50)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x≡27\\,(\\text{mod}\\,50).$",
